    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d733">

                <pb facs="0633" n="621" xml:id="pg621"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890E.01/146: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gwynn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-23T21:00:00+02:00">August 23, 1942—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 24—9:09 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>302. I left with de Gaulle today a paraphrase of the Department’s 136, August
                        21.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d733-f1"/> De Gaulle will soon address me a note
                    containing his observations. He expressed his great satisfaction that the United
                    States Government is taking an active interest in the present difficulty. He
                    said he was somewhat pained that the Department appeared somewhat to doubt
                    sincerity of the French spheres [<hi rendition="#italic">promises of?</hi>]
                    independence. It is quite possible that he will request American mediation.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Cairo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gwynn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
